      "blocks": {
        "material_block_1": {
          "type": "material_block",
          "settings": {
            "title": "About 1/8 the weight of cotton.",
            "body": "<p>EthicalDown Kapok™ is hollow at its core, so it's exceptionally light — and warm.</p>",
            "image_url": "https://ucarecdn.com/dea0315a-8b99-48e9-b01e-e5cc3d6e4cef/",
            "image_caption": "",
            "image_position": "full"
          }
        },
        "material_block_2": {
          "type": "material_block",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Down that grows on trees.",
            "body": "<p>Being plant-based, kapok “breathes” in a way synthetic fiber can't — absorbing moisture and generating warmth as it does. Sometimes called “white silk cotton,” it's blended with recycled polyester and formed into a sheet: EthicalDown Kapok™.</p>",
            "image_url": "https://ucarecdn.com/a21ea9da-ddb5-4657-ab56-b163a7666035/-/format/auto/-/preview/3000x3000/-/quality/lighter/",
            "image_caption": "",
            "image_position": "full"
          }
        },
        "material_block_3": {
          "type": "material_block",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Animal-friendly, by nature.",
            "body": "<p>Traditional down comes from waterfowl feathers — roughly 30 birds' worth per jacket, by some estimates. Kapok comes from a seed pod, not a bird, so there's nothing here to feel conflicted about.</p>",
            "image_url": "https://ucarecdn.com/7195ca1b-4376-4352-a81c-f6aad630623d/-/format/auto/-/preview/3000x3000/-/quality/lighter/image%20139.png",
            "image_caption": "▲ The kapok seed pod",
            "image_position": "full"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["material_block_1", "material_block_2", "material_block_3"],
      "settings": {
        "section_id": "a25-material",
        "bg_color": "#faf9f6",
        "label": "The Material",
        "heading": "<p>One material, <em>years in the making: kapok.</em></p>",
        "intro": "KAPOK KNOT is built around a single material — kapok, a plant-based fiber grown across Southeast Asia. Its short fibers made it notoriously hard to spin, limiting it to life-jacket stuffing and cushions for decades.\n\nAfter years of joint development, we created EthicalDown Kapok™ — a patent-pending insulation sheet that finally solves that problem."
      }
    },
